## 1. Introduction
As a major disease that seriously endangers human health, cancer prevention and control has become one of the key global health strategies [1–4]. Chemotherapy is still the most basic and most important treatment for malignant tumors [5]. Drug carriers for controlled release can reduce the side effects on normal cells and prevent them from being decomposed in the circulation in the body [6–11]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) exhibit unique advantages in drug delivery due to their large specific surface area, porous structure, and ease of functionalization [9,12]. With the development of research, it was found that the structure, drug loading and fluorescence of MOFs composed of mixed metal ions or ligands can be controlled by adjusting the ratio of different metal ions or ligands [13–16], so as to achieve the best drug loading and release, and the optimal fluorescence for imaging-guided therapy.
However, long-term chemotherapy will inevitably cause drug resistance and reduce the efficacy of treatment [17]. Hypoxia in the tumor microenvironment (TME) is considered to be the direct cause of drug resistance, and regulating the hypoxic environment of the TME can be expected to reduce drug resistance [18–22]. Fenton reaction can catalyze excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in tumors to produce $0_{2}$ and ∙OH. The produced $0_{2}$ can regulate the hypoxia and the ∙OH can kill cancer cells [23–27]. Therefore, catalytic therapy base on Fenton reaction is expected to improve treatment efficiency, continuously producing $0_{2}$ and ∙OH through the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle [28–30], shown as following:
$$
\mathrm{Fe^{2+}+H_{2}O_{2}\ +\ H^{+}\longrightarrow H_{2}O\ +\ F e^{3+}+\cdot O H}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}+\cdot00\mathrm{H}+\mathrm{H}^{+}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Fe^{3+}\ ^{+}\ ^{-}O O H\ \longrightarrow\ F e^{2+}\ ^{+}O_{2}\ ^{+}\ H^{+}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{OOH}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\to\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\cdot\mathrm{OH}
$$
Iron ion-centered MOFs (Fe-MOFs) are commonly used in the Fenton reaction to degrade organic pollutants [31]. However, since the optimum pH for Fenton reaction is 2.0–4.5, the Fenton reaction is limited by the tumor microenvironment $\mathrm{(pH~}5.8–6.5\mathrm{)}$ ) [32–34]. Therefore, it is necessary to increase the Fenton reaction rate of Fe-MOFs. It was found that doping with some transition metal ions (3d metal ions) with variable valence, such as ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ [35– 36], can accelerate Fenton reaction to generate more ∙OH. It was also found that Fenton reaction can be increased by doping rare earth ions (4f metal ions) [37–39], as the coupling (f-d) between lanthanides and transition metal ions is strong, and combining these two spin carriers into a single material can produce better performance [40]. At the same time, rare earth materials are widely used in bioimaging, luminescent devices and quantitative analysis of biological samples [41–42]. Therefore, 3d-4f MOFs are expected to improve Fenton response and enable visualization of tumor therapy.
In order to further improve catalytic efficiency, photothermal materials were used to accelerate the Fenton reaction, increasing the temperature of the tumor site with near-infrared irradiation [43–45] Therefore, synthesis of a kind of multifunctional MOFs with both photothermal and Fenton catalytic properties can maximize the Fenton reaction rate while enabling synergistic treatment of photothermal and catalytic therapy.
Nitrogen-containing organic ligands are the earliest ligands for construction of MOFs [45], which can satisfy the coordination affinity of 3d and 4f metal ions because of its multidentate chemical properties [37,46]. Besides, the carboxyl group (–COOH) can provide rich coordination modes, easily to be completely deprotonated. Combined with the rigid structure of the benzene ring, it is beneficial to synthesize materials with novel coordination structures and strong stability. In particular, the donor–acceptor (D-A) structure composed of electron-donating groups and electronwithdrawing groups can transfer energy within the molecule with the excitation of external conditions, and is expected to produce photothermal effects [47–48].
Inspired by this, Fe/Tm-MOFs were constructed using 2- methylimidazole and trimesic acid as mixed ligands, and iron ions and thulium ions as mixed metal ions, as shown in Scheme 1. The rigid structure formed by 3d/4f metal ions and two organic ligands facilitated the easy transport of electrons, brings certain photothermal properties, and accelerates the Fenton reaction. At the same time, MOFs can be used as carriers for DOX due to their porous structure. As a result, Fe/Tm-MOFs can achieve synergistic therapy of photothermal therapy, catalytic therapy and chemotherapy assisted with the guidance of bio-imaging because of $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , building a simple ‘‘all in one” multifunctional treatment platform.
## 2. Experimental methods
## 2.1. Materials
Chemicals and solvents were purchased from Aladdin Reagent (Shanghai) Co., Ltd and used as received without further purification. All reagents are of analytical grade. Thulium(III) Chloride Hexahydrate, ferric(III) chloride hexahydrate, trimesic acid, 2- methylimidazole, doxorubicin hydrochloride, $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethyl benzidine (TMB), 2,7-dichlorofluorescin diacetate(DCFH-DA), methanol, ethanol. fresh double distilled water was used throughout the experiment.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Fe/Tm-MOFs
Iron chloride and thulium chloride were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and the mixture was stirred at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{min}$ (as solution A). Trimesic acid and 2-methylimidazole were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol, followed by stirring at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ (as solution B). Then, the solution B was slowly added into solution A, and the mixture was stirred at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ before transferring to a Teflon-lined autoclave for a reaction of $24\mathrm{h}$ at $160^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The other five kinds of Fe/TmMOFs were synthesized using similar procedure with some modification of the ratio of ligands, as shown in Table1.
## 2.3. Characterization
The infrared thermal images were recorded by a thermal imager (Ningbo Yuanming Laser Technology Co., Ltd). The power X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were performed using a D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer (Bruker Company, USA). The UV– Vis spectra were recorded on a spectrophotometer (Shanghai INESA Instrument Co., Ltd). FT-IR spectra were recorded by using a Single frequency infrared spectrophotometer (PerkinElmer, USA). Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were captured on a JSM6510LV scanning electron microscope (JEOL, Japan). The dynamic light scattering was measured by Nanoseries Zetasizer (Malvern, UK).
## 2.4. Detection of extracellular hydroxyl radical
$_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine(TMB) reagent was selected as an indicator to determine the generation of OH. The TMB in water was added with Fe/Tm-MOFs, followed by the NIR light irradiation. The TMB solution with and without Fe/Tm-MOFs was used as the control groups. After light irradiation, the changes of the UV–Vis spectra and the color of the solution were recorded.
## 2.5. Photo-thermal effect and thermal stability of Fe/Tm-MOFs
$\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ with different ligand ratios were dispersed in aqueous solutions and were irradiated by $808\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser $(1.5~\mathsf{W}\vert$ $\mathsf{c m}^{2}.$ ) for $10~\mathrm{\min}$ . The $\mathrm{Fe/Tm-MOFs}$ (0, 100, 200, 300, 400, $500~\mathrm{ppm}.$ in aqueous solution with ligand ratio of 1:1 and optimized particle size and morphology were irradiated by $808\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser $(1.5~\mathrm{W/cm}^{2})$ to examine the temperature changes. In order to further investigate the photothermal stability, heating–cooling cycles were measured on Fe/Tm-MOFs. The variation of temperature was recorded by a digital thermistor temperature sensor. Thermographs of different solutions were recorded with an infrared thermal camera.
## 2.6. Drug loading and drug release
Fe/Tm-MOFs ( $\mathrm{100mg},\$ were added into a flask containing $20~\mathrm{mL}$ doxorubicin (DOX) aqueous solution $(0.1\ \mathrm{g/L})$ . After being placed at room temperature in dark for $24\mathrm{h}$ , $48\mathrm{~h~}$ and $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the DOX loaded Fe/Tm-MOFs were collected by centrifugation and the residual DOX in the solution was measured by measuring the UV–Vis absorbance at $460~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
To investigate the DOX release, the DOX-loaded ${\mathrm{Fe/Tm}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ particles were placed into a dialysis bag $(\mathrm{MWCO}=3500)$ ) and then immersed in $25~\mathrm{\mL}$ of PBS solution at different values of pH $\mathrm{\Phi_{\cdot}p H}=7.4$ and 5.8) at a temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ At different time point, the absorbance of DOX $(\lambda_{\mathrm{abs}}=460\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) in PBS buffer solution was measured by UV–Vis spectrometer and the concentration of the released DOX is calculated.
## 2.7. Cytotoxicity measurement of Fe/Tm-MOFs
4T1 human cervical carcinoma cells was grown in DMEM medium supplemented with $10\%$ FBS and $1\%$ penicillin/streptomycin in an incubator with a humidified air atmosphere of $5\%\thinspace{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The 4 T1 cells $5\times10^{3}$ cells/well) were seeded onto 96-well plates. After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of incubation, the cell culture medium was replaced with fresh medium containing 25, 50, 100, 150, $200~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ of $\mathsf{F e}/$ Tm-MOFs/DOX and Fe/Tm-MOFs compounds, respectively. The cells were then incubated for another $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ before the addition of $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ basal medium containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ CCK8. After incubation at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ , the absorbance of each well at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ was tested by a plate reader and the cell proliferation of cells was calculated.
## 2.8. Detection of Intracellular OH
4 T1 cells were seeded in a 6-well plate. After incubation in DMEM medium for $12{\mathrm{~h~}}$ the medium was replaced with a fresh medium containing $200~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Fe/Tm-MOFs and incubated for another $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For the control group, the cells were irradiated with NIR $(808\ \mathrm{nm}\ 1.5\ \mathrm{W/cm^{2}})$ laser for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ . After that, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DCFHDA $(1\ \mathrm{mg/mL})$ was added into each well, and then the cells were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for another $20\mathrm{min}$ in dark. The intracellular fluorescence images of each well were then recorded by a laser confocal microscope.
## 2.9. Imaging
Fluorescence Imaging: 4 T1 cells were seeded on coverslips in a 6-well plate. When the cell density reached $50\%$ , Fe/Tm-MOFs was added to the culture medium and the cells were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an incubation of $5\%\thinspace{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The cells were washed three times with PBS $\mathrm{\pH}=7.4\$ ) before imaging by a laser confocal scanning microscope.
Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI): $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of cell suspension was subcutaneously injected into female mice (body weight $18-$ $20{\mathrm{~g}}{\mathrm{,}}$ in the thigh area to establish a mouse model with subcutaneous tumors. After 15 days’ inoculation, mice (number: 2) were used for imaging studies on a 3 T MRI scanner (Siemens Magnetom Trio $3.0~\mathrm{T}$ ). Then, $0.15{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}$ -MOFs solution was injected into the mice through the tail vein. $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -weighted MRI was performed sequentially at 0, 1, 2, 3, and $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ after intravenous injection.
## 2.10. In vivo therapy
The 4 T1-tumor bearing mice were established by subcutaneous injection of 4 T1 cells. The tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into 3 groups when the tumor volume reached about 50– $100~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ . Mice in Group I and Group II were injected with
$150~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of PBS and Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX $(0.5~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ in PBS via tail vein, respectively. Mice in Group III were injected with $150~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $(0.5~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 on the first day, after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ the tumor site injected with Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX was irradiated with infrared light. Changes in tumor volume and body weight of mice were recorded.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Fe/Tm-MOFs
Fe/Tm-MOFs with different ligand ratio were synthesized by hydrothermal method. The structures of Fe/Tm-MOFs were affirmed by X-ray diffraction analysis (Fig. 1(a)). The diffraction peaks of Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 (1:1) were appeared at $24.02^{\circ}$ , $33.25^{\circ}$ , $35.49^{\circ}$ , $40.80^{\circ}$ , 49 $0.75^{\circ}$ , $54.48^{\circ}$ , $62.88^{\circ}$ , $64.28^{\circ}$ . The strong diffraction peaks of Fe/Tm-MOFs-1、Fe/Tm-MOFs-2 and Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 were shown at $33.25^{\circ}$ and $35.49^{\circ}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe/Tm-MOFs{\mathrm{-}}{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{}-$ MOFs-5 shown at $11.72^{\circ}$ and $16.48^{\circ}$ . The varied position of the diffraction peak of each MOFs is attributed to the different ligand ratio. The XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ , Fe- $\cdot\mathrm{L}_{1}/\mathrm{L}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Tm}{-}\mathrm{L}_{1}/\mathrm{L}_{2}$ and dual-ligands were shown in Figure S1. The change in the position of the diffraction peak indicates that the interaction between the metal ion and the ligand forms a new crystal plane.
Fourier transform infrared spectrum (FT-IR) of $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ at different ligand ratio was measured. As shown in Fig. 1b, the broad adsorption band located at $3500{-}3000{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ was mainly due to the stretching vibrations of $-\mathsf{O H}$ group. The adsorption bands corresponding to –COOH group were not found in the range of $1800-$ $1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of Fe/Tm-MOFs-3, suggesting the deprotonation of BTC ligands. Four adsorption peaks appeared in range of 1650– $1550~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1470{-}1390\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which could be assigned to the asymmetric vibration and symmetric vibration of the carboxylate groups. The FT-IR data also implied that the BTC have coordinated with metal ions. As shown in Figure S2, the bands in range of $2900{-}2700\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the characteristic groups of 2- MeIm’s NAH. The adsorption bands were not found in the sample of $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ , implying the coordination of 2-MeIm with metal ions. The bands at 1006 and $532~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were also observed, corresponding to the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bending modes and metal oxygen stretching vibration. The bands at $1107\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $757-721~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to stretching vibrations of $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ groups and the deformation vibrations of the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ groups in the benzene ring.
The morphology of Fe/Tm-MOFs at different ratio of the ligand was observed by SEM images. As shown in Fig. 2, the morphology of Fe/Tm-MOFs-1 and Fe/Tm-MOFs-2 are obviously elliptical, which is different from the cubical $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOFs}{-}3$ and $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{}-$ MOFs-4 and Fe/Tm-MOFs-5, and that of the BTC and 2- methylimidazole (Figure S3). The size is about $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{(Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}}$ MOFs-1), $850~\mathrm{\nm}$ (Fe/Tm-MOFs-2), $500~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ (Fe/Tm-MOFs-3), $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fe/Tm-MOFs-4), and ${900}\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fe/Tm-MOFs-5). DLS measurements were employed to further evaluate the particle size and polydispersity (Fig. 3(a)). Due to the Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 has the optimal particle size, the biological application was carried out using this particle. The formation of Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 was also confirmed by EDX analysis (Figure S4). It is clear that the Fe/TmMOFs-3 particle has C, N, O, Tm, Fe elements, indicating the successful synthesis of Fe/Tm-MOFs. The stability of Fe/Tm-MOFs $^{-3}$ in buffer with different pH was evaluated by recording the change of particle size. As shown in Figure S5, negligible changes of the particle size were obtained in different buffers, indicating the good stability of Fe/Tm-MOFs-3.
Furthermore, zeta potentials of Fe/Tm-MOFs were measured and the data were presented in Fig. 3(b). All Fe/Tm-MOFs present negative charge in aqueous solution that can avoid protein adsorption when used as drug carriers and increase the circulation time in blood. In addition, the fluorescence properties of ${\mathrm{Fe/Tm}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ were shown in Fig. 4(a). With the excitation wavelength of $345~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , all $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ have emission peaks at $380~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Moreover, the Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 exhibits the highest fluorescence intensity, which could be attributed to the effective energy transfer between the metal ion and the ligand [49], as also shown in in Fig. 4(b), where the mechanism of Fe/Tm-MOFs’ fluorescence was presented.
## 3.2. Analysis of photo-thermal performance
The photo-thermal performance of Fe/Tm-MOFs was explored and the result was displayed in Fig. 5. The photo-thermal properties of monometallic mono-ligand, monometallic double ligand and bimetallic mono-ligand were analyzed, and the results were shown in Table S1. The samples of Fe- $\cdot\mathrm{L}_{1}$ , Fe- ${\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{L}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}_{1}$ , Fe/Tm$\mathrm{L}_{1}/\mathrm{L}_{2}(1{:}1)$ $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{L}_{1}$ : BTC; $\mathsf{L}_{2}$ : 2-methylimidazole) at the same concentration were irradiated with $808\ \mathrm{nm}$ NIR light for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the changes of temperature were recorded. The results revealed that the temperature changes are 10.8, 8.1, 13.5, and 22.6 for $\mathrm{Fe-L}_{1}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{L}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}_{1}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}_{1}/\mathrm{L}_{2}(1{:}1)$ , respectively. The temperature change clearly indicates that the $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}_{1}/\mathrm{L}_{2}$ has the best photothermal performance. Interestingly, as shown in Fig. 5(a), higher photo-thermal performance was noticed for the particle with increasing proportion of $\mathrm{L}_{1}(\mathrm{BTC})$ . With the increase of BTC’s proportion, the formation of a $\pi{-}\pi$ conjugated framework and a highly electronically delocalized structure facilitated the conversion of the light energy to other energy, such as heat in this work [50–51].
It is also clear that under the NIR light irradiation for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the temperature changes of $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ with a ratio of 3:1, 2:1, 1:1 are much larger than the limit temperature changes of cancer cell’s survival. Taking all in consideration, Fe/Tm-MOFs with a ratio of 1:1 ligand ratio was selected to explore the relationship between temperature-dependent concentrations. As shown in Fig. 5(b), the temperature changes are dependent on the increase of the Fe/Tm-MOFs’ concentration (Fig. 5(c)). Moreover, Fe/Tm-MOFs showed excellent photostability upon a continues NIR $\left(808\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ 1 light irradiation. As shown in Fig. 5(d), after NIR light irradiation continuously for four cycles, the process of temperature changes did not show obvious difference. The UV–Vis-NIR also indicated that the Fe/Tm-MOFs have photothermal properties for PTT applications (Figure S6).
An infrared thermal imaging was used to record the temperature change during laser irradiation. As shown in Figure S7, the temperature was quickly increased for $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOFs}{-}3$ under NIR light irradiation, suggesting the excellent photo-thermal performance. The photo-thermal efficiency of Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 was then calculated to be $34.26\%$ through recording the temperature changes upon heating and cooling processes (Figure S8). In comparison with the recently reported photo-thermal materials (Table S2), such a high photo-thermal efficiency $(34.26\%)$ implies that the Fe/Tm-MOFs have excellent photo-thermal performance.
## 3.3. Analysis of the OH generation
In this experiment, ∙OH was generated through the continuous circulation of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ , which was confirmed by the XPS spectra of Fe 2p of the Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 (Figure S9). The peaks at $709.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of the Fe(II) cation and the peaks at $723.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ were ascribed to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of the $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ cation; the peaks at $711.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and the peaks at 725.6 eV were ascribed to Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of the $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ cation, respectively. These results demonstrate the presence of both $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ and $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ in Fe/Tm-MOFs-3 for ∙OH generation. TMB, as an indicator for OH detection [52], can be oxidized by generated OH to present blue color in the solution (Figure S10). To confirm the $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ doping promoted OH generation, qualitative and quantitative results of chromogenic reaction of TMB was performed. As shown in Figure S11, the sample with Fe/Tm-MOFs showed quickly color changes than that of Fe-MOFs. Through recording the absorbance value of TMB at $650~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , the quantitative analysis of OH generation was conducted. In comparison with $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ , the TMB with Fe/Tm-MOFs exhibited larger absorbance, indicating the higher level of OH being produced. This is because the $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ doping can promote electron transfer and further facilitate the generation of OH. The promoted electron transfer was also confirmed by EIS Nyquist plots. As shown in Figure S12, Fe/Tm-MOFs exhibited a smaller semicircle than Fe
MOFs within a high-frequency region in the EIS Nyquist plots, which was ascribed to the electron transfer on the surface of the catalyst [53–54].
Figure S13(a) showed the effect of photothermal performance on the generation of OH. It can be seen that the solution with Fe/Tm-MOFs turns blue and the blue color of the solution deepened with NIR irradiation. It proved Fe/Tm-MOFs can catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate OH and NIR can accelerate the production of OH. The mechanism was shown in Figure S13(b).
4T1 cells with different treatments were stained with $^{2^{\prime},7^{\prime}}$ -dic hlorofluorescin diacetate (DCFH-DA), a fluorescent probe for OH (Fig. 6) [55]. Compared with the Fe/Tm-MOFs group, significant elevation of OH level was observed in the group treated with NIR irradiation, suggesting the high levels of OH generation with NIR laser irradiation. The mean fluorescence intensity exposed with $\mathrm{Fe/Tm}{}-$ MOFs $^+$ NIR was higher than that of $\mathrm{Fe/Tm\mathrm{-}M O F s}$ . The results further corroborate the NIR promoted catalysis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to OH, which is in agreement with previous report by Mei’s group [56].
## 3.4. Drug loading and drug release
DOX as an anthracycline antibiotic widely used for cancer chemotherapy [57]. The feasibility of Fe/Tm-MOFs as the potential carrier for DOX was further explored by nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms (Figure S14). The specific surface area of Fe/TmMOFs is $7.9877~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ the adsorption average pore diameter is $6.8955\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The DOX loading was also investigated by FT-IR and fluorescence spectra analyses. As shown in Figure S15, the peak position change indicated by shaded area and the change of DOX fluorescence intensity before and after drug loading confirmed successful DOX loading. The drug release profile was evaluated under diverse stimuli such as pH and NIR light, as shown in Fig. 7(a). The results showed that about $20\%$ of preloaded DOX was released from the Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX over $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ . However, over $43\%$ of DOX was released from the Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX at $\mathsf{p H}5.8$ .
The higher rate of DOX release in acidic condition could be attributed to the protonation of hydroxyl groups, which resulted in the hydrogen bond dissociates and the release of the DOX. Upon NIR laser irradiation for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the drug release rate was significantly enhanced. Such NIR-induced drug-release could be attributed to the local increased temperature produced by Fe/Tm-MOFs owing to their efficient NIR light conversion into heat, which might result in the destruction of certain bonding. When exposed to a momentary NIR laser irradiation $\cdot10\mathrm{min})$ , the drug release rate was significantly enhanced. The drug release profile of Fe-MOFs control group was also evaluated under the same condition with Fe/TmMOFs/DOX (Figure S16). It was found that a slight increase exposed to NIR, indicating that Fe-MOFs have no thermal-responsive, further confirm doping $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ into Fe/Tm-MOFs can produce better photo-treatment performance. The mechanism of release DOX was shown in Figure S17.
## 3.5. Cytotoxicity analysis
In order to investigate the effect of drugs released from Fe/TmMOFs/DOX and synergetic therapy of chemotherapy and catalytic therapy, the cells viabilities were examined. As shown in Fig. 7 (b), in comparison with Fe/Tm-MOFs, the Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX showed significant inhibition of the cell growth. As the concentration of particles increases, the survival rate gradually decreases and the Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX group has a significant decrease, the phenomena can be explained that with the increase of the concentration of Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX, more drug molecules are released in cells, killing more cancer cells.
## 3.6. In vivo therapy
Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX suspension was injected into tumors of mice with $4\mathrm{T}1$ tumors, then the laser irradiation $808~\mathrm{nm}$ , $1.5~\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2\cdot}$ was applied for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The control group was injected with PBS $(150~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and with the same laser irradiation process. The infrared thermal imaging camera was used to record the infrared thermal image of the mouse, as shown in Figure S18. It is clear that the temperature of the tumor site is gradually increased, indicating that the Fe/Tm-MOFs can be accumulated at the tumor sites, and can be used for photothermal therapy with the trigger of NIR.
Encouraged by in vitro results of Fe/Tm-MOFs, the therapeutic effect in vivo was evaluated. The mice were randomly divided into three groups, the control group was injected with PBS, as shown in c1-c3; the treatment group I was injected with DOX loaded Fe/TmMOFs (Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX), as shown in d1-d3; the treatment group II was injected with Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX and was irradiated with NIR for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , as shown in e1-e3. The weight and tumor size of mice in each group were measured within 10 days from the laser irradiation at the first day. The results were shown in Fig. 8(a) and (b). The weight of the mice in the three groups did not change significantly, indicating that the mice treated with Fe/Tm-MOFs and laser irradiation has no obvious side effects. The mice in the control group showed a significant increase in tumor size; while the mice treated with Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX (treatment group I) showed a subtle increase in tumors, remaining basically unchanged, indicating that the Fe/Tm-MOFs/DOX can inhibit the growth of tumors. It is worth noting that the tumors of the mice treated with Fe/TmMOFs/DOX and NIR irradiation (treatment group II) were significantly reduced, indicating that the co-treatment has a significant anti-cancer effect. In the process of synergistic treatment, we noticed that the tumor site of the mice in treatment group II turned black at the second day. The reason was that the high temperature caused by photo-thermal burns the tumor epidermis. Therefore, it shows that Fe/Tm-MOFs have excellent photo-thermal performance, which can be further proved by the representative photos of these mice on the second day, fifth day and tenth day (Figures c1-c3, d1-d3, e1-e3).
## 3.7. Imaging performance
In order to verify the fluorescence imaging ability of Fe/TmMOFs, 4 T1 cells were incubated with Fe/Tm-MOFs (Figure S19). The fluorescence intensity increased with increasing concentration of Fe/Tm-MOFs. In vivo MRI performance of Fe/Tm-MOFs was also verified and the images were presented in Figure S20. After that injection, the tumor site in mice changed from bright to dark and bright again over the time. The time of the $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ imaging contrast agent circulating in the body and the new metabolism in the mouse body will affect the intensity of the contrast agent imaging. Therefore, Fe/Tm-MOFs can be used for ${\sf T}_{2}$ -weighted MRI imaging.
## 4. Conclusion
This work introduced a kind of multifunctional MOFs to maximize the Fenton reaction rate while enabling synergistic treatment of photothermal and catalytic therapy. The photothermal and catalytic performance can be controlled by ratio of doping rare earth, thereby maximizing the Fenton reaction through photothermal accelerated catalysis, so as to solve the limitations of the tumor microenvironment on Fenton reaction [32–34]. In contrast to the reported materials for NIR-enhanced Fenton reactions, this work only requires a simple hydrothermal synthesis [43–45]. Besides, combination with the imaging performance, Fe/Tm-MOFs is successfully used as an ‘‘all-in-one” tumor treatment platform, and the in vivo results demonstrate that DOX loaded Fe/Tm-MOFs can achieve an amazing therapeutic effect of synergy therapy in only 10-day treatment period. Compared with the reported complicated multi-step preparation of ‘‘all-in-one” tumor treatment platforms [58–60], this work provides a simple and novel design for bioimaging-guided synergistic treatment of photothermal therapy, catalytic therapy, and chemotherapy.
